<header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">Japan · early Bosch years</p><h1>The chapter that changed my standards.</h1><p class="lede">I spent roughly a year of my early career travelling between India and Yokohama. I went for ECU projects. What stayed with me was a way of taking ordinary things seriously.</p></header>
<article class="prose">
<div class="photo-pair"><figure><img src="/images/japan/friends-yokohama.jpg" alt="Venkat with friends in Yokohama"/><figcaption class="photo-caption">YOKOHAMA</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="/images/japan/friends-tokyo-street.jpg" alt="Venkat with colleagues and friends in Japan"/><figcaption class="photo-caption">TOKYO</figcaption></figure></div>
<p>I was in my early twenties and had barely travelled outside India. Japan reset a number of assumptions I did not know I had: that cities must be noisy, meetings naturally start late, a few minutes do not matter, and “good enough” is a normal endpoint.</p>
<p>The engineers around me were careful without making a performance out of being careful. Commitments were treated as commitments. Small defects were worth fixing. The most experienced people in a room did not need to dominate it.</p>
<h2>Reliability as a culture, not a feature.</h2><p>It is easy to turn Japan into a collection of business-book words: kaizen, craft, punctuality. What mattered to me was seeing those ideas in mundane behaviour. A train arriving when it said it would. Somebody returning something you dropped. An engineer finishing the unexciting part of a job properly.</p>
<div class="pull">I came home with a much lower tolerance for the sentence “that’s probably fine.”</div>
<p>Years later, I can see the connection between that experience and why I am drawn to reliability problems. Embedded software, industrial ML and AI agents are wildly different technologies, but they all punish the gap between what a system appears to do and what it actually does under pressure.</p>
<h2>The people mattered more than the lesson.</h2><p>Some of my closest memories are not technical at all: small apartments, cheap dinners, travelling with other young Indian engineers, and Japanese colleagues who became friends rather than contacts. That is why I keep this page. It is part of the professional story, but not reducible to one.</p>
